Z||000007||theogre||08-19-2000||04:51 PM||||You'll likely want to replace the door. It's easier and more reliable than straightening the framing of it.<P>As long as it wasn't hit in front so hard that the frame bent then it looks like the rest is all bolt on parts. A replacement nose will be a pain but an older nose will work in a pinch until you find the 87-88 one.||||reg||1
Z||000008||LarryB||08-19-2000||04:57 PM||||Awwww..man. It hurts to see that. Was it a hit and run? ||||reg||1
Z||000009||mrfiero||08-20-2000||02:57 AM||||That stinks! <P>A similar event happened to me last summer. <BR>My friend, his girlfriend and myself were <BR>off to Vegas....he had a burgundy Formula <BR>and I was in my white Formula.  On the way <BR>there (driving up Vail pass) he lost his <BR>transmission.  We had it towed back and his <BR>girlfriend took a bus while we continued on <BR>in my Formula (he was in a pool tournament <BR>and needed to be there ASAP).  While in <BR>Vegas I managed to win $5000 in a card <BR>game.  I decided to buy his Formula, since <BR>he wouldn't have the $$ to fix it up.  It <BR>sat out in front of his house for almost 2 <BR>months (I had no where to put it).  I had it <BR>potentially sold to another friend who owns <BR>a transmission shop......he was to look at <BR>it/pick it up on a Sunday.  In the wee hours <BR>on the day before (Saturday) a drunk driver <BR>slammed into the drivers door.  It pushed it <BR>hard into the curb and actually bent the <BR>right rear wheel and suspension!  <P><BR>It was a shame, because all it needed was a <BR>new tranny.....I still have it, but I've <BR>been selling off the parts when I can.<P><BR>It appears yours might be fixable.....my <BR>incident took out the B pillar area, which <BR>essentially totals any car.  At least yours <BR>took a "normal" looking hit.<P><BR>I do have lots of misc parts, so LMK if I <BR>can help you out in any way.  I have an '84 <BR>coupe, '85 SE, '86 SE, '88 Formula and <BR>an '88 coupe for parts + access to 3 dozen <BR>or so Fieros in junkyards around here.<P><BR>Take it easy.<P>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 08-20-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 08-20-2000).]||||reg||8
Z||000010||Vader||08-20-2000||07:31 AM||||Mr. Fiero,<P>Your phone might be ringing soon...thanks for the tip.<P>Tha guy who hit it actually left a note.  There are still some honest, decent people here in the midwest.  I just hope his insurance company is as forthright as he was.<P>So far I'm seeing a cracked hood, nose, fender, door, and some steering/suspension damage.  I may have to get those tubular control arms I've been looking at.<P>Later,<BR>Vader<P>------------------<BR>"<I>Make Me Bad</I>"||||reg||1
Z||000011||Gokart||08-20-2000||07:33 AM||||That SUCKS  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> It wouldn't say it is the end though Vader. Your ride is beautiful and is well worth restoring. Just hope the insurance settlement will take care of all your costs. Good luck, Marc.<p>[This message has been edited by Gokart (edited 08-20-2000).]||||reg||8
Z||000012||mrfiero||08-20-2000||01:07 PM||||Wow!  They actually left a note!?  That is amazing!  All I got was a phone call from my friend telling me the Fiero was trashed (I thought he was kidding).<P>At any rate,  LMK what you need.....I have the body panels you mentioned and I have a complete front suspension too (unless you decide to go tubular).<P>I really hope the insurance company doesn't string you along.  I was T-boned in an '88 GT about 5 years ago and even though it was clearly her fault (ran a red light) it took me 6 months and a lawyer to get any money.  ||||reg||6
Z||000013||theogre||08-20-2000||01:39 PM||||WARNING! DO NOT allow the insurance company to "total" the car if you plan to fix it. Being what it is that is most likely what they will try to do.<P>If they total it, after which they own it, and you then buy it back, you will end up with a salvage tittle. YOU DON'T WANT THAT! A car with a salvage tittle usually is worth less. In many states it also has to pass a "special" inspection before it can be returned to the road. You also may not be able to get insurance for it or have to pay more.||||reg||1
Z||000014||mrfiero||08-20-2000||01:47 PM||||This is true.  My GT, even though it was too screwed up to fix, did not have a salvage title.  The insurance company never owned it.....I could have conceivably fixed it without the stigma of a salvage title.<P><p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 08-20-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||JohnnyK||08-20-2000||03:16 PM||||If all else fails, I also have the nose and hood and some doors of an 84.||||reg||1
Z||000016||wahoo4u||08-21-2000||03:35 PM||||I have a 88 formula at home that is being parted out along with 2 84's all but the rear bumper is intact on the 88 have all panels for an 84 (both are coupes) my e-mail is wahoo4u@excite.com.  if you can't find a nose i have no need for it my cars all have the aero nose on them.  all of these cars were never hit so the frame is in good shape on them.  let me know what you need and we could work something out.  to bad about the incident.||||reg||9
